{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If a man takes a vow to HaShem, etc.</b> One needs to know why He repeated, saying <b>takes a vow or swears an oath</b>, and was not satisfied to say 'if he takes a vow to HaShem or swears.' Further, why did He repeat, saying <b>he shall not profane</b>, etc., <b>according to all that proceeds from his mouth</b>? Now, since there are two kinds of vows and oaths — one concerning matters that touch the soul, which divides into two categories: one, to perform a good deed, such as to learn and to teach, to consecrate, for deeds of kindness; and one, to distance oneself from abomination, to fence oneself off not to dwell near a suspected woman, not to walk in the company of workers of iniquity and the like — and secondly, concerning matters touching the body in deeds that involve neither prohibition nor commandment — and this too divides into two: to eat and drink, to dwell in such-and-such a dwelling and the like, or not to eat a certain thing and not to wear this garment and the like — HaShem legislates His laws as a statute, what to do, and said ״כִּי יִדֹּר נֶדֶר״ — an unspecified vow, indicating the act of vowing to go to a certain place or to do a certain thing. And because there is a vow that is לַה׳ ('to HaShem') whose law differs from ordinary vows, as the verse will explain, therefore He specified and said ״נֶדֶר לַה׳״. You thus find that Scripture mentions two kinds of vows: profane vows and sacred vows." "And His saying <b>or swears an oath</b> corresponds to abstention from action, as He concludes ״לֶאֱסֹר אִסָּר עַל נַפְשׁוֹ״. And in this too there are two kinds of vows: profane vows, such as forbidding things in which there is no commandment in their prohibition, or also forbidding things that are already forbidden — such as Boaz, who swore to his inclination." "And HaShem showed wisdom in that He placed the word <b>לַה׳</b> between 'vow' and 'oath,' to attach 'to HaShem' to both vow and oath, to say that the repetition that appears in vow and oath is a specification touching HaShem and not its inclusion in the general category of profane vows, because its law differs. And this is what He concludes: <b>he shall not profane his word, according to all that proceeds</b>, etc. — meaning: concerning vows that are profane matters and oaths that are restraint from things in which there is no requirement of a commandment, such as the oath of Boaz and the like, He said ״לֹא יַחֵל״ — meaning, he himself shall not profane, but others may permit it for him, as our Sages of blessed memory explained (Nedarim 14a). And concerning vows that are vows of consecrations and vows of heaven and oaths in which there is a requirement of a commandment, He said <b>according to all that proceeds from his mouth he shall do</b> — in these there is no condition that others can release, but they are things that have no release." "<b>Further,</b> it appears one may explain the reason for His saying <b>לַה׳</b> based on what Rambam wrote in chapter 11 of the Laws of Vows, and this is his language: 'A minor, a son of twelve years, who swore or vowed — they examine him: if he knows in whose name he vowed,' etc., end quote. And this is what HaShem intended in the statement <b>נֶדֶר לַה׳</b>, for since He preceded and said <b>אִישׁ</b> ('a man'), He comes to set a benchmark that one be called a 'man' for this matter, and said ״נֶדֶר לַה׳״ — meaning, when he knows in whose name he vowed. And likewise regarding an oath, as He said ״אוֹ הִשָּׁבַע שְׁבֻעָה״ — that the law is the same for an oath."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "In comment 0, the second paragraph begins ב׳ (a scribal abbreviation for 'the second'), referring back to the earlier enumeration of two categories; rendered as 'secondly.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:04.132238471Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}